# =============================================================================
# BirdNet-Behavior — Docker environment variables
#
# QUICK START
#   1. cp .env.example .env
#   2. Uncomment and fill in the REQUIRED section below (lat/lon + one
#      audio source). See "why some keys are commented out" below.
#   3. docker compose up -d       ← base compose, works for RTSP/file-watch
#      …or an overlay for live mic capture:
#        docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.alsa.yml  up -d
#        docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.pulse.yml up -d
#   4. Open http://localhost:8502
#
# The BirdNET+ V3.0 model (~541 MB) is downloaded automatically on first run —
# from the same GitHub release line as the image, sha256-verified, with Zenodo
# as the fallback. You do not need to pick or fetch a model yourself.
#
# Settings with no env var (detection confidence threshold, sensitivity,
# per-species thresholds, email, rare-bird quarantine) live in the web UI at
# http://localhost:8502/admin/settings.
#
# WHY SOME KEYS BELOW ARE COMMENTED OUT
#   Uncomment a key to use it, and always give it a value. A key left in place
#   with an empty value is NOT the same as leaving it unset: the daemon's
#   argument parser reads a blank environment variable as a *supplied* value,
#   so `BIRDNET_LATITUDE=` means "the latitude is the empty string" and refuses
#   to start rather than falling back to the default. Every optional key is
#   therefore shipped commented; `docker/entrypoint.sh` strips blanks as a
#   backstop, but `docker compose exec … --doctor` reads this file's values
#   directly and gets no such protection.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
#   REQUIRED — edit these three things before first run
# =============================================================================

# --- 1. Station location -----------------------------------------------------
#   Used for the solar recording schedule, species frequency filter,
#   BirdWeather uploads, and the dashboard sunrise/sunset markers.
#
#   Format: decimal degrees (NOT degrees-minutes-seconds). Negative numbers
#   mean South latitude or West longitude. Easiest way to find your numbers:
#     1. Open https://www.openstreetmap.org/
#     2. Right-click your house / garden / station location
#     3. Choose "Show address" — the two numbers at the top are lat,lon.
#
#   Examples:
#     Boston, MA, USA          → BIRDNET_LATITUDE=42.3601   BIRDNET_LONGITUDE=-71.0589
#     Berlin, Germany          → BIRDNET_LATITUDE=52.5200   BIRDNET_LONGITUDE=13.4050
#     Sydney, Australia        → BIRDNET_LATITUDE=-33.8688  BIRDNET_LONGITUDE=151.2093
#
#   Validation: latitude must be between -90 and 90, longitude between -180
#   and 180. Run `birdnet-behavior --doctor` after editing to verify.
#BIRDNET_LATITUDE=
#BIRDNET_LONGITUDE=

# --- 2. Audio source — set exactly ONE of the following ---------------------

# Option A — USB / ALSA microphone (most Raspberry Pi users)
#   Find the device:  arecord -l
#     → "card 1: Device [USB Audio], device 0:"   →   plughw:1,0
#   Start with:
#     docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.alsa.yml up -d
#BIRDNET_ALSA_DEVICE=

# Option B — RTSP stream (IP camera / network audio)
#   Works with the base docker-compose.yml — no overlay needed.
#     docker compose up -d
#BIRDNET_RTSP_URL=

# Option C — PulseAudio / PipeWire source (desktop Linux)
#   Use "default" for the system default source, or a name from:
#     pactl list short sources
#   Start with:
#     docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.pulse.yml up -d
#BIRDNET_PIPEWIRE_DEVICE=

# --- 3. Which image to pull --------------------------------------------------
#   latest   — the current release. DuckDB behavioral analytics (activity
#              sessions, resident-vs-migrant, dawn-chorus validation, …) is
#              built into EVERY image and on by default — there is no separate
#              "-analytics" tag. Pin a version in production, e.g. 0.5.0.
BIRDNET_IMAGE_TAG=latest


# =============================================================================
#   OPTIONAL — safe defaults, edit only if you want to change behaviour.
#   You can leave this entire section untouched and the container will work.
# =============================================================================

# --- Web server --------------------------------------------------------------
# Host port for the web UI (container always listens on 8502 internally).
BIRDNET_PORT=8502
# Custom site name shown in the web UI header.
#BIRDNET_SITENAME=
# Species info link target: ebird | allaboutbirds | none
BIRDNET_INFO_SITE=ebird
# Web UI authentication (HTTP Basic). Viewing the dashboard is always open; only
# the /admin panel (settings, backups, software update) is gated by a password.
# Unlike the bare-metal installer, Docker does NOT auto-generate one — so until
# you set CADDY_PWD here, /admin is open to the whole LAN (BIRDNET_LISTEN below
# binds 0.0.0.0). Set a password, or front it with a reverse proxy / VPN. User
# defaults to "birdnet". See docs/SECURITY_HARDENING.md.
CADDY_USER=
CADDY_PWD=
# --- HTTPS -------------------------------------------------------------------
# How the dashboard terminates TLS: off | self-signed | manual
#
#   off          Plain HTTP (the default, and what every earlier release did).
#                Fine on a trusted LAN, or behind a reverse proxy that already
#                terminates TLS.
#   self-signed  Generates a small local CA and a server certificate it signs,
#                under BIRDNET_TLS_DIR. Browsers warn until you import the CA
#                file once (the startup log prints its path); a later
#                certificate rotation does not bring the warning back.
#   manual       Serves BIRDNET_TLS_CERT / BIRDNET_TLS_KEY. Both are re-read
#                when they change on disk, so an ACME client renewing them at
#                03:00 is picked up without a restart.
#
# `--doctor` checks all of this before startup does.
#BIRDNET_TLS_MODE=off
# Certificate chain and private key (PEM), for BIRDNET_TLS_MODE=manual.
#BIRDNET_TLS_CERT=/etc/ssl/birdnet/fullchain.pem
#BIRDNET_TLS_KEY=/etc/ssl/birdnet/privkey.pem
# Where HTTPS listens. Defaults to BIRDNET_LISTEN's host on port 8503, so plain
# HTTP keeps answering on 8502. Set this equal to BIRDNET_LISTEN to serve only
# HTTPS on the one port.
#BIRDNET_TLS_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8503
# Answer plain HTTP with a 308 redirect to HTTPS instead of serving the app.
# Ignored (with a warning) when HTTPS and HTTP share one socket.
#BIRDNET_TLS_REDIRECT=1
# Names the generated certificate covers, comma-separated. Defaults to
# localhost, this machine's hostname, <hostname>.local, the bound address and
# 127.0.0.1. Changing this regenerates the server certificate (not the CA).
#BIRDNET_TLS_HOSTNAMES=birds.example.com,192.168.1.40
# Where the generated CA and server certificate live. Defaults to a `tls`
# directory beside the database.
#BIRDNET_TLS_DIR=/data/tls
# How many days a generated server certificate is valid for. The CA that signs
# it lives ten years regardless, so rotation never invalidates a trust import.
#BIRDNET_TLS_DAYS=397

# Extra seconds of audio before each detection in its saved clip.
#
# A clip is centred on its detection: with the default 6-second EXTRACTION_LENGTH
# and BirdNET's 3-second window that is 1.5 seconds either side. This adds to
# the front only, so 1.0 makes the lead-in 2.5 seconds and the clip 7 seconds.
#
# Worth having because a call is not centred in the window that detects it. A
# bird that started singing half a second before the analysed chunk opened has
# its first notes in the previous one — audible to a person deciding whether the
# identification is right, and exactly what an asymmetric lead-in recovers.
#
# Clips now span segment boundaries, so this reaches real audio even for a
# detection at the very start of a segment. It is refused across a gap in
# recording: a station that restarted, or a source that dropped, gives a shorter
# clip rather than one splicing two different times together.
#
# 0 (the default) keeps clips centred, exactly as before.
#PRE_CAPTURE_SECS=1.0

# Species tracking: first of the year, first of the season, returning.
#
# The season a date falls in depends on the station's latitude — March is
# spring in the north and autumn in the south, and near the equator there are
# wet and dry seasons rather than four temperate ones. Set BIRDNET_LATITUDE (or
# the latitude in Settings) and the station works this out; leave it unset and
# it reports no season at all rather than guessing a northern one.
#
# The tracking year defaults to the calendar year. A station that counts its
# year from the start of the breeding season can move the reset, and a date
# before it then belongs to the tracking year that began the previous time
# around. These are settings-table keys rather than environment variables
# because they belong with the station's other operator settings:
#
#   tracking_year_reset_month   1-12,  default 1
#   tracking_year_reset_day     1-31,  default 1
#
# "Returning after an absence" shares its threshold with the rare-species feeds
# (rare_species_days, default 30) so the RSS feed and the day list cannot
# disagree about which birds are notable.
#
# Read it at GET /api/v2/species/tracking?date=YYYY-MM-DD[&notable_only=true].

# Dynamic per-species confidence threshold. Off by default.
#
# A fixed confidence threshold settles two different questions with one number:
# "is this a bird at all" and "is this bird plausible here". Once a Tawny Owl
# has been recorded in the wood at 0.9, a later 0.45 Tawny Owl is very probably
# another Tawny Owl; a 0.45 for a species never recorded within 500 km is not.
# Raising the threshold to suppress the second suppresses the first as well.
#
# When enabled, a species is confirmed by a detection at or above the trigger
# that survived EVERY other gate — the privacy and noise filters, the species
# occurrence (geomodel) filter, the plausible-hour filter, the corroboration
# filter, and the threshold itself. Each confirmation lowers that species'
# threshold one step (x0.75, x0.50, x0.25), floored, and the level lapses after
# the configured hours without reinforcement. A single singing bout counts once:
# a species can advance at most one level per fifteen minutes.
#
# Enabling this also lowers the threshold the classifier itself runs at, to the
# floor below — otherwise the detections it exists to recover are discarded
# inside the model and the feature reaches nothing. The extra cost is in
# post-processing, not inference.
#BIRDNET_DYNAMIC_THRESHOLD=1
# Confidence at or above which a surviving detection confirms its species.
# Should sit well above the global threshold: the point is that the station was
# sure, not merely that it accepted the detection. Default 0.80.
#BIRDNET_DYNAMIC_THRESHOLD_TRIGGER=0.80
# Hard floor. No species goes below this whatever its level. This is the number
# that bounds the damage if the reasoning above is wrong for your site.
# Must be BELOW the global confidence or nothing can be adjusted; the station
# warns at startup if it is not. Default 0.25.
#BIRDNET_DYNAMIC_THRESHOLD_MIN=0.25
# How long a level survives without reinforcement, in hours. Default 24.
#BIRDNET_DYNAMIC_THRESHOLD_HOURS=24

# Sound-level calibration, in dB SPL at digital full scale.
#
# Unset means the station reports dBFS: negative, relative to its own gain, and
# not comparable with another station or a published figure. That is fine for
# every question about change at one place over time — is it getting louder, is
# the microphone dying, when does the dawn chorus start — which is what the
# third-octave series is for.
#
# To calibrate, play a known level at the microphone (a 94 dB SPL acoustic
# calibrator at 1 kHz is the usual instrument), read what /api/v2/soundlevel
# reports for the 1 kHz band uncalibrated, and set this to
# 94 minus that reading. Readings then come back as dB SPL and say so; the unit
# travels with the data, and each stored hour records the offset in force when
# it was measured, so a series that spans a calibration has a visible seam
# rather than a silent one.
#BIRDNET_SPL_CALIBRATION_DB=

# Reverse proxies whose forwarded client-IP headers may be believed.
#
# Comma-separated CIDR blocks, bare addresses, and the reserved names
# `loopback`, `private` and `cloudflare`. Loopback is always trusted whether or
# not it is named — a proxy on the same host is not a stranger.
#
# The default is `private`: loopback plus RFC1918, unique-local and link-local.
# That covers the two common topologies (a proxy on this host, a proxy on
# another box on the LAN) and is why a proxied station records each visitor
# separately rather than filing the whole household under the proxy's address.
#
# When the peer is NOT in this list, forwarded headers are ignored completely
# and the connection's own address is used. That is what stops a directly
# exposed station from being told who its callers are by the callers.
#
# Set `loopback` to trust nothing but this machine. Add `cloudflare` when the
# station sits behind Cloudflare, so the right-to-left walk of
# `X-Forwarded-For` passes the edge and reaches the real visitor instead of
# stopping at one of a few hundred Cloudflare addresses.
#
# A value that does not parse is not ignored: it is logged and the station
# falls back to loopback only.
#BIRDNET_TRUSTED_PROXIES=private
#BIRDNET_TRUSTED_PROXIES=private,cloudflare
#BIRDNET_TRUSTED_PROXIES=loopback
#BIRDNET_TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.0/8,192.168.1.5

# Serve the station from under a path, e.g. https://home.example/birdnet
#
# For the common home setup: one hostname, a reverse proxy, several services
# under different paths. Without this the station needs its own subdomain or
# its own port. Home Assistant ingress works this way too.
#
# Empty (the default) serves from the root and changes nothing.
#
# The prefix is applied to every URL the station emits — links, form actions,
# HTMX targets, redirects, the session cookie's Path, and the WebSocket URLs
# the live pages build in the browser. Your proxy should pass the full path
# through *unchanged* rather than stripping it: the station expects to see
# /birdnet/today, not /today.
#
#   Caddy:  handle_path is wrong here; use `handle /birdnet/*` and
#           `reverse_proxy localhost:8080` with no path rewriting.
#   nginx:  location /birdnet/ { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080; }
#           (note: no trailing path on proxy_pass, or nginx strips the prefix)
#
# A visitor who reaches the host root is redirected to the prefix, so a
# mistyped bookmark lands on the station rather than on a 404.
#
# A value that does not parse (a `..` segment, whitespace, a query string) is
# refused and logged, and the station serves from the root — it is not
# silently corrected, because a corrected prefix will not match what the proxy
# sends and every request then 404s with nothing in the log to say why.
#BIRDNET_BASE_PATH=/birdnet

# Cross-origin (CORS) allow-list. Empty = same-origin only (the secure default).
# Set a comma-separated list of origins ONLY if you call the API from another
# host, e.g. https://dashboard.example.com
#BIRDNET_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Secret used to sign cookie sessions on the new branded login page (O-14).
# Set a random value of 32+ bytes so sessions survive restarts and process
# moves. If unset, the secret is derived deterministically from CADDY_PWD
# (rotating the password signs out every existing session — the intended
# semantics). If both are unset, a per-process random secret is used and
# every outstanding session invalidates on restart. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
BNB_SESSION_SECRET=
# Cookie session lifetime in days, default 14, range 1-365. "Remember me"
# extends the issued cookie to 90 days regardless of this value.
BNB_SESSION_TTL_DAYS=14
# Absolute base URL of this station (e.g. https://birds.example.com). When
# the URL starts with https://, the session cookie is issued with Secure;
# on a plain-HTTP LAN this stays off so browsers send the cookie back.
# Reuses the existing BNB_BASE_URL below if you've set that already.
BNB_PUBLIC_URL=

# --- Sharing, feeds & station coordinates (web features) ---------------------
# Secret used to sign public share links (/r/<token>) with HMAC-SHA256. Set a
# random value of 32+ bytes so links survive restarts. If left blank a random
# per-process secret is used and every outstanding share link is invalidated on
# restart (fail-secure). Generate one with: openssl rand -base64 48
BNB_SHARE_SECRET=
# Absolute base URL of this station as seen by the outside world. Used to build
# absolute links in the RSS/iCal feeds and share pages (e.g. http://birdnet.local
# or https://birds.example.com). Defaults to http://localhost:8502 if unset.
BNB_BASE_URL=
# Station latitude/longitude used for the dawn-chorus sunrise/sunset overlay on
# /analytics/dawn-chorus. Falls back to BIRDNET_LATITUDE/LONGITUDE, then to a
# conservative 05:30/20:00 if neither is set. Same values feed the weather
# poll loop below — locale-tolerant (42,3601 works the same as 42.3601).
BNB_STATION_LAT=
BNB_STATION_LON=

# --- Weather overlay (O-23) --------------------------------------------------
# Off by default — a fresh install does NOT contact api.open-meteo.com until
# you opt in. Set BNB_WEATHER_ENABLED=1 to start a background task that pulls
# the hourly forecast every 30 minutes and paints it as a signal-context band
# on the day-strip and dawn-chorus charts. The moon-phase badge is always
# shown regardless (it's a local calculation).
#
# Privacy-sensitive operators can self-host Open-Meteo and point the client
# at it via BNB_WEATHER_BASE_URL (e.g. http://open-meteo.lan); the upstream
# default is the free api.open-meteo.com tier.
BNB_WEATHER_ENABLED=
BNB_WEATHER_BASE_URL=

# --- Multi-stream RTSP (overrides BIRDNET_RTSP_URL) --------------------------
#   Comma-separated; each URL gets its own capture pipeline.
#BIRDNET_RTSP_URLS=

# --- Network / privacy ---------------------------------------------------------
# Two connections are made with no configuration: a daily release check against
# api.github.com, and Wikipedia species-image downloads. Everything else is off
# until you configure it. See docs/book/getting-started/configuration.md.
#
# Any non-empty value turns off BOTH of the above, so the station makes no
# outbound connection you did not ask for. Integrations you configured
# explicitly (Apprise, BirdWeather, MQTT, SMTP, heartbeat, weather) keep working.
#BIRDNET_OFFLINE=
# Non-empty = skip only the daily release check.
#BIRDNET_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=

# --- Recording schedule ------------------------------------------------------
# Seconds per captured segment, range 3–600 (default 15 is a good balance).
BIRDNET_SEGMENT_DURATION=15
# Schedule mode:
#   all-day                — capture audio 24/7 (default)
#   solar                  — sunrise to sunset only (needs lat/lon)
#   fixed:HH:MM-HH:MM      — local-time window (e.g. fixed:06:00-20:00)
BIRDNET_RECORDING_SCHEDULE=all-day
# Non-empty value = suppress recording during night hours (needs lat/lon).
#BIRDNET_NIGHT_INHIBIT=
# Minutes offset from sunrise/sunset for twilight recording. Applies to both
# ends of the day unless one of the two below overrides it for its own end.
BIRDNET_TWILIGHT_OFFSET=30
# Set either of these to give dawn and dusk different windows — the dawn chorus
# usually wants a longer lead-in than the evening needs a tail. Unset falls back
# to BIRDNET_TWILIGHT_OFFSET. Both are also editable at /admin/settings.
#BIRDNET_PRE_SUNRISE_OFFSET=
#BIRDNET_POST_SUNSET_OFFSET=

# --- Detection / inference ---------------------------------------------------
# Note: confidence threshold and sensitivity are configured in /admin/settings.
# Analysis window overlap, 0.0–2.9 seconds (higher = more sensitive, more CPU).
BIRDNET_OVERLAP=0.0
# Seconds of audio saved around each detection (BirdNET-Pi: EXTRACTION_LENGTH).
# Also editable at /admin/settings. 1-60; longer clips verify better by ear and
# cost proportionally more disk.
#BIRDNET_EXTRACTION_LENGTH=6

# --- Logging ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Global level: trace, debug, info, warn, error, off. RUST_LOG overrides this.
#BIRDNET_LOG_LEVEL=info
# Per-subsystem levels, e.g. `audio=debug,web=warn`. Names are operator-facing:
# audio, detection, web, db, integrations, analytics. An explicit Rust path
# (birdnet_core::inference=trace) also works. Unknown names are reported at
# startup rather than ignored.
#BIRDNET_LOG_MODULES=

# --- Species occurrence filtering -------------------------------------------
# The occurrence ("geo") filter drops species that do not occur near the station
# at this time of year. It needs THREE things and is inert without all three:
#
#   1. station coordinates (BNB_STATION_LAT / BNB_STATION_LON, or the dashboard)
#   2. the geomodel file               -> BIRDNET_METADATA_MODEL
#   3. that model's own label file     -> BIRDNET_METADATA_LABELS
#
# The entrypoint downloads (2) and (3) on first start and sets both variables
# for you, so leave them blank unless you are staging your own. Set
# BIRDNET_METADATA_MODEL yourself and the entrypoint leaves the geomodel
# entirely alone. If the ~14 MB download fails the container still starts and
# detects; it just does not filter by location, and says so.
# `birdnet-behavior --doctor` reports which of the three is missing.
#
# The geomodel and the classifier do NOT score the same species list (12 012
# against the V3.0 Global 11K classifier's 11 560), so the label file is what
# maps one to the other and the two are matched by scientific name. Leave it
# blank only for a metadata model indexed identically to the classifier -- a
# matched BirdNET pair -- which the station verifies at startup and refuses if
# it does not hold.
#BIRDNET_METADATA_MODEL=
#BIRDNET_METADATA_LABELS=
# Point the geomodel fetch at a mirror (air-gapped / bandwidth-limited sites):
#BIRDNET_GEOMODEL_BASE=
# Occurrence threshold, range 0.0–1.0. No effect while the filter is off.
#   Higher  = stricter: only species highly likely at your station/season.
#   Lower   = looser:  more rare/migrant species allowed through.
#   Default 0.03 is BirdNET-Pi's recommended starting point.
BIRDNET_SF_THRESH=0.03
# Human-voice privacy filter, range 0.0–1.0.
#   0.0  = disabled (no privacy filtering — fastest).
#   0.02 = recommended starting point if voices are being captured.
#   0.10 = aggressive; may suppress some quiet bird calls too.
BIRDNET_PRIVACY_THRESHOLD=0.0
# Discard an audio chunk a dog barked in (0.0 = disabled, typical 0.5-0.8). A
# bark is broadband, and the classifier also scores whatever species it most
# resembles — so the bark becomes a bird that was never there.
BIRDNET_NOISE_THRESHOLD=0.0
# Non-bird label names to watch, comma-separated. Unset means Dog; an empty
# value means watch nothing. Others in the label set: Siren, Engine,
# Power tools, Fireworks, Gun, Environmental, Noise — the last two score highly
# on ordinary quiet recordings, so watching them suppresses a great deal.
#BIRDNET_NOISE_CLASSES=
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Offsite backups
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The weekly snapshot lives beside the database, on the same card. That covers
# a corrupt page and a bad import; it does not cover the card failing, the
# enclosure flooding, or the Pi being stolen. Set this and each snapshot is
# encrypted on the station and uploaded. Off by default.
#
#   off  | s3 (AWS, B2, R2, Wasabi, MinIO, Ceph, Garage) | sftp (any SSH host)
BIRDNET_OFFSITE_BACKUP=off

# The passphrase every backup is encrypted under. There is NO way to turn
# encryption off, and NO recovery without this: it is not stored anywhere, not
# derivable from the backup, and not known to anyone else. Write it down
# somewhere that is not this station. At least 12 characters.
#
# Deliberately no command-line flag: an argument is visible in `ps` to every
# user on the machine and is copied into the journal by systemd.
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_PASSPHRASE=

# How many backups to keep at the destination. 0 keeps everything. Only files
# this station wrote are ever removed.
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_KEEP=8

# --- S3-compatible ---------------------------------------------------------
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_S3_BUCKET=my-birdnet-backups
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_S3_PREFIX=stations/garden
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_S3_REGION=eu-west-2
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_S3_SECRET_KEY=
# auto picks by endpoint: virtual-host for AWS, path for everything else.
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_S3_ADDRESSING=auto

# --- SSH file server -------------------------------------------------------
# Key authentication only; passwords are disabled because a batch upload
# cannot answer a prompt and would hang until something killed it.
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_SFTP_HOST=backup.example.net
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_SFTP_PORT=22
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_SFTP_USER=birdnet
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_SFTP_DIR=/srv/backups/garden
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_SFTP_IDENTITY=/var/lib/birdnet/ssh/id_ed25519
# Defaults beside the identity file.
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_SFTP_KNOWN_HOSTS=/var/lib/birdnet/ssh/known_hosts
# `yes` or `accept-new`. There is no `no`: host key checking is what makes the
# upload go to your server rather than to whoever answers.
#BIRDNET_OFFSITE_SFTP_HOST_KEY_POLICY=yes

# Require a species to be heard in more than one nearby analysis window before
# it is recorded: off, lenient, moderate, balanced, strict. A real bird sings
# across several windows; a classifier artefact usually fires once. Each level
# is a fraction of the windows within six seconds — 20%, 30%, 50%, 70% —
# rounded up.
#
# This does NOTHING without BIRDNET_OVERLAP: with no overlap a six-second
# neighbourhood is two 3-second windows, and 20% of two rounds to one, which
# every detection already meets. `--doctor` reports the overlap each level
# needs. Off by default because it removes rows.
BIRDNET_CONFIRMATION_LEVEL=off
# Suppress a repeat of the same species within this many seconds (0 = off).
# A 15-second recording is five 3-second chunks, so a bird singing throughout
# is recorded five times, and every count in the app is a row count. Off by
# default because switching it on changes how many rows a station records.
BIRDNET_DUPLICATE_INTERVAL_SECS=0
# Quarantine day birds heard in the middle of the night. Owls, nightjars,
# rails, bitterns and thick-knees are exempt by genus. Needs station
# coordinates. Detections are quarantined for review, never dropped, because
# the taxonomy is genus-level and cannot be complete.
BIRDNET_NIGHT_FILTER=0
# Minutes after sunset / before sunrise before the night window opens. Birds
# sing through dusk and start again well before sunrise.
BIRDNET_NIGHT_MARGIN_MINS=60
# Extra genera or scientific names always allowed at night, comma-separated.
# A station recording nocturnal flight calls (migrating thrushes and warblers
# calling overhead) should leave the filter off or name those genera here.
#BIRDNET_NIGHT_EXTRA_NOCTURNAL=
# Non-empty = enable SNR / spectral flatness / rain-wind rejection.
#BIRDNET_QUALITY_FILTER=
BIRDNET_QUALITY_MIN_SNR=3.0
# Species name translation language: en | de | fr | es | pt | it | ja | zh | …
BIRDNET_LANG=en

# --- Notifications -----------------------------------------------------------
# Delivered in-process, no Apprise installation involved. Apprise URL syntax,
# comma- or newline-separated. Natively supported schemes:
#   discord://  slack://  tgram://  ntfy://  gotify://  pover://  json://
#   (and ntfys:// gotifys:// jsons://)
#BIRDNET_NOTIFY_URLS=
# For any other scheme, Apprise is still used: either its API server...
#BIRDNET_APPRISE_URL=
# ...or the `apprise` CLI with a config file. When every URL in that file is
# one of the schemes above, the CLI is not invoked at all.
#BIRDNET_APPRISE_CONFIG=
# Ceiling on notifications per destination per minute; 0 disables it. The cap
# is about the services' limits, not ours: Discord allows 5 requests/second per
# webhook, Telegram ~30/second, and Pushover 10,000 messages a *month*.
BIRDNET_NOTIFY_RATE_PER_MINUTE=12
BIRDNET_NOTIFY_CONFIDENCE=0.8
# Trigger mode: each | new-species | new-species-daily
BIRDNET_NOTIFY_TRIGGER=each
#BIRDNET_NOTIFY_SPECIES_EXCLUDE=
#BIRDNET_NOTIFY_SPECIES_ONLY=
#BIRDNET_NOTIFY_TITLE_TEMPLATE=
#BIRDNET_NOTIFY_BODY_TEMPLATE=
# Weekly report day: monday | tuesday | … | sunday | disabled
BIRDNET_WEEKLY_REPORT_SCHEDULE=monday

# --- Detection deadman (end-to-end "is it actually detecting?" watchdog) ------
# Hours of zero detections before a warning is logged and (if Apprise is
# configured) one alert is sent, with a recovery notice when birds return.
# A station that has never detected anything never alarms. 0 disables the
# alert but still exports the birdnet_detection_silence_seconds gauge.
# Raise it for genuinely sparse habitats. Default: 24.
BIRDNET_DEADMAN_HOURS=24

# --- Station health (the faults the deadman cannot see) ----------------------
# The deadman answers "is the station detecting at all?". This answers the
# faults a station keeps detecting straight through, so the deadman stays quiet:
#   * an audio source down while others keep recording
#   * the disk full enough that recordings are being purged to make room
#   * the CPU at or above its throttling point (80 C on a Pi)
#   * a backup or integrity check that has not completed in three weeks
# One alert per episode with a recovery notice when it clears, through the same
# Apprise notifier as the deadman, and only after three consecutive five-minute
# polls so a mic that re-enumerates or a momentary disk spike stays quiet.
# Default: true.
BIRDNET_STATION_HEALTH_ALERTS=true

# --- BirdWeather -------------------------------------------------------------
#BIRDNET_BIRDWEATHER_TOKEN=
# Self-hosted ingest endpoint override. Leave blank for the public
# app.birdweather.com map. Set to your own endpoint implementing the
# BirdWeather station API (only the host changes; the /stations/<token>/...
# path shape is preserved) to keep observation locations under your own
# governance — e.g. for rare or endangered species where a public map is a
# poaching risk. The offline queue and ordered replay come with it.
#BIRDNET_BIRDWEATHER_URL=

# --- MQTT (Home Assistant, Node-RED, Mosquitto, …) ---------------------------
#BIRDNET_MQTT_HOST=
# Leave unset for 1883, or 8883 when BIRDNET_MQTT_TLS is on.
#BIRDNET_MQTT_PORT=
BIRDNET_MQTT_CLIENT_ID=birdnet-behavior
# Connect over TLS. The certificate is always verified — there is no way to
# turn that off. Setting BIRDNET_MQTT_CA_FILE turns TLS on by itself.
#BIRDNET_MQTT_TLS=
# PEM file of extra trust anchors. For a broker behind a private CA this is the
# *CA's* certificate; for a self-signed broker it is the broker's own. Either
# way the broker's certificate must carry CA:FALSE — plain `openssl req -x509`
# sets CA:TRUE, which rustls refuses from a server.
#BIRDNET_MQTT_CA_FILE=
# Hostname to check the certificate against, when connecting by IP.
#BIRDNET_MQTT_TLS_SERVER_NAME=
#BIRDNET_MQTT_USERNAME=
#BIRDNET_MQTT_PASSWORD=
BIRDNET_MQTT_TOPIC_PREFIX=birdnet
#BIRDNET_MQTT_RETAIN=
#BIRDNET_MQTT_HA_DISCOVERY=

# --- Heartbeat (uptime monitoring ping URL) ----------------------------------
#BIRDNET_HEARTBEAT_URL=

# --- Storage / retention -----------------------------------------------------
# wav | mp3 | flac | ogg (non-WAV needs ffmpeg or sox in the image)
BIRDNET_AUDIO_FORMAT=wav
# Maximum extracted recordings kept per species (0 = unlimited).
BIRDNET_MAX_FILES_PER_SPECIES=0
# Reclaim the audio of detections older than this many days (blank/0 = keep
# audio forever, the default). Detection records are always kept — only the
# sound file goes — and locked clips are exempt.
#BIRDNET_CLIP_RETENTION_DAYS=
# Frequency shift applied to saved clips in Hz (0 = disabled, try 1000–4000).
BIRDNET_FREQ_SHIFT_HZ=0
# Comma-separated paths the disk manager must never auto-purge.
#BIRDNET_DISK_EXCLUDE=
# Disk-usage % at which the oldest recordings start being purged (blank = 95).
# Locked clips are never purged, whatever this is set to.
#BIRDNET_DISK_PURGE_THRESHOLD=
# Seconds a raw capture segment is kept in the temporary streaming folder
# before being cleared (blank = 600). Only needed until the detector has read
# it; the default is far longer than that.
#BIRDNET_STREAM_RETENTION_SECS=
# Hard ceiling in MB on that same streaming folder; oldest segments go first
# (blank = 512). It is usually RAM-backed, so this stops a busy station
# filling it.
#BIRDNET_STREAM_MAX_MB=
# Directory with custom species images (overrides Wikipedia cache).
#BIRDNET_CUSTOM_IMAGE_DIR=

# Where species photographs come from: `wikipedia` (default) or `flickr`.
#
# Wikipedia needs no key and covers most species, but for a long tail it has no
# photograph at all — and for many others it has a museum skin, an egg, or a
# range map.
#
# `flickr` searches Flickr first and falls back to Wikipedia when Flickr has
# nothing, so choosing it can only add coverage. It needs an API key, free from
# https://www.flickr.com/services/apps/create/apply/
#
# Only the Creative Commons licences that permit commercial use are requested
# (CC BY, CC BY-SA, CC BY-ND, CC0, Public Domain, Flickr Commons, US Government
# Work). Your station's page may be published, and the station cannot know
# whether that counts as commercial, so the permissive set is the safe one.
# Every photograph is shown with its photographer's name and a link to the
# Flickr page, which is what those licences require.
#
# FLICKR_FILTER_EMAIL restricts the search to one photographer's photostream —
# set it to your own Flickr account's email address to show your own
# photographs of the birds your own station heard. Wikipedia still fills in the
# species you have not photographed.
#
# The key is masked in support bundles; so is the local part of the address.
#IMAGE_PROVIDER=wikipedia
#FLICKR_API_KEY=
#FLICKR_FILTER_EMAIL=


# =============================================================================
#   ADVANCED — internal container paths, custom models, local builds.
#   Most users should leave this entire section blank.
# =============================================================================

# Container paths (correct by default; change only if you remap the volume).
BIRDNET_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8502
BIRDNET_WATCH_DIR=/data/recordings
# Species image cache. Defaults to an `images/` dir beside the database when
# unset (stock installs show photos out of the box); set empty to disable.
BIRDNET_IMAGE_CACHE_DIR=/data/cache
BIRDNET_ANALYTICS_DB=/data/analytics.db
BIRDNET_MODEL_DIR=/data/model

# DuckDB memory cap for behavioral-analytics queries (default 256MB). Raise it
# on a roomy box for faster large-window queries; keep it low on a 2–4GB Pi.
BIRDNET_DUCKDB_MEMORY_LIMIT=256MB

# Bring-your-own model (leave blank — the entrypoint auto-downloads BirdNET+
# V3.0 from GitHub (Zenodo fallback) on first run; set these ONLY if you have a
# custom model).
#BIRDNET_SKIP_MODEL_DOWNLOAD=
#BIRDNET_MODEL=
#BIRDNET_LABELS=
#BIRDNET_LABELS_DIR=
# (BIRDNET_METADATA_MODEL / BIRDNET_METADATA_LABELS are documented under
#  "Species occurrence filtering" above — they are not part of the classifier
#  download, and nothing auto-downloads them.)

# Host UID whose PulseAudio/PipeWire socket the pulse overlay mounts
# (run `id -u` on the host if your UID is not 1000).
PULSE_UID=1000

# Local builds only — pass `BUILD_FEATURES=analytics` to `docker compose build`
# to compile the analytics variant yourself instead of pulling it from GHCR.
BUILD_FEATURES=
